[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
```js
// ❌ Mapbox GL JS (or old MapLibre v5) default import – no longer works in v6
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';

// ✅ MapLibre GL JS v6 – namespace import (or import only what you need)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// or, using named imports
import { Map, NavigationControl } from 'maplibre-gl';
```

### TL;DR  
In v6 you **don’t listen for `styleimagemissing` and call `addImage()` inside the handler** – that no longer resolves the request.  
Instead register a **missing-style-image resolver** with `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()`.  
The resolver is called whenever the style asks for an image that isn’t in the sprite; you fetch / generate the image there and add it to the map with `map.addImage()`. After the image is added the style will automatically pick it up.

---

## 1. What changed?

| v5 (pre-v6) | v6 (≥ 6.0.0) |
|------------|-------------|
| `map.on('styleimagemissing', e => { map.addImage(e.id, …) })` – the handler *resolved* the missing request. | `styleimagemissing` is now **notify-only**. Adding an image inside the handler does **not** satisfy the pending request. |
| No extra API needed. | Use **`map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(resolverFn)`**. The resolver must add the image itself (via `map.addImage`). Its return value is ignored. |

> **Why?** The new resolver gives MapLibre a single place to async-resolve missing images (e.g. fetch from a CDN, generate on-the-fly, read from IndexedDB) without the race-conditions that the old event-based pattern caused.

---

## 2. Basic pattern (v6)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';   // or named imports
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://example.com/my-style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

/**
 * Resolve a missing style image.
 *
 * @param {string} id – the image name the style asked for.
 * @returns {Promise<void>}
 */
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  try {
    // 1️⃣  Load or generate the image.
    //    Here we fetch a PNG from a CDN, but you could also draw on a canvas,
    //    read from IndexedDB, etc.
    const response = await fetch(`https://my-icons.example.com/${id}.png`);
    if (!response.ok) throw new Error('404');

    const blob = await response.blob();
    const img = await createImageBitmap(blob);   // works in browsers that support it

    // 2️⃣  Add the image to the map.
    //    `addImage` will replace any existing image with the same id.
    map.addImage(id, img);
    // No need to return anything – MapLibre will automatically re-evaluate the style.
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn(`Missing-icon resolver: could not load "${id}"`, err);
    // Optional: add a fallback placeholder so the layer doesn’t disappear.
    const placeholder = generatePlaceholderCanvas(id);
    map.addImage(id, placeholder);
  }
});

/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
/* Helper – generate a tiny placeholder if the fetch fails (optional) */
function generatePlaceholderCanvas(id) {
  const size = 32;
  const canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
  canvas.width = canvas.height = size;
  const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');
  ctx.fillStyle = '#ff00ff';
  ctx.fillRect(0, 0, size, size);
  ctx.fillStyle = '#000';
  ctx.font = '10px sans-serif';
  ctx.textAlign = 'center';
  ctx.textBaseline = 'middle';
  ctx.fillText('?', size / 2, size / 2);
  return ctx.getImageData(0, 0, size, size);
}
```

### What the code does

1. **Create the map** as usual.
2. **Register a resolver** with `setMissingStyleImageResolver`.  
   The resolver receives the *image id* that the style tried to use.
3. Inside the resolver you can:
   * **Fetch** a remote PNG/SVG/WEBP.
   * **Generate** an image on-the-fly (canvas, SVG string → `ImageBitmap`, etc.).
   * **Read** from a cache (IndexedDB, Service Worker, local storage…).
4. **Add the image** to the map with `map.addImage(id, image)`.  
   Once the image is added, MapLibre automatically re-evaluates the style and the newly-added icon appears.
5. (Optional) **Fallback** – if the image can’t be loaded you can add a placeholder so the layer stays visible.

---

## 3. Full-featured example (fetch + cache)

If you want to avoid re-fetching the same icon many times, cache the result:

```js
const iconCache = new Map(); // id → ImageBitmap

map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // Return early if we already have it.
  if (iconCache.has(id)) {
    map.addImage(id, iconCache.get(id));
    return;
  }

  // Try to fetch the image.
  const url = `https://my-icons.example.com/${id}.png`;
  const resp = await fetch(url);
  if (!resp.ok) throw new Error(`Icon ${id} not found`);

  const blob = await resp.blob();
  const img = await createImageBitmap(blob);

  // Cache and add.
  iconCache.set(id, img);
  map.addImage(id, img);
});
```

---

## 4. What if you’re still on v5?

If you *haven’t* upgraded to v6 yet, the old pattern still works:

```js
map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => {
  // e.id is the missing image name
  fetch(`https://my-icons.example.com/${e.id}.png`)
    .then(r => r.blob())
    .then(b => createImageBitmap(b))
    .then(img => map.addImage(e.id, img));
});
```

**But** as soon as you move to v6 that handler will no longer resolve the request, so replace it with the resolver shown above.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **Map stays blank / icons never appear** | `styleimagemissing` handler used in v6 (doesn’t resolve) | Switch to `setMissingStyleImageResolver`. |
| **`ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED`** when `require('maplibre-gl')` | Using CommonJS with v6 (ESM-only) | Convert the file to ESM (`import …`) or load dynamically (`await import('maplibre-gl')`). |
| **Image flickers or appears late** | Resolver adds the image *after* the style has already rendered a frame. | This is expected; the map will re-render automatically once `addImage` is called. If you need it earlier, pre-load the icons before creating the map. |
| **`addImage` throws “Image already exists”** | Resolver called twice for the same id (e.g., rapid style changes). | Guard with a cache (`map.hasImage(id)`) or the `iconCache` pattern above. |
| **Worker fails to load in a bundler** | Forgetting `setWorkerUrl()` (bundler case). | See the “Bundler-only `setWorkerUrl()`” section of the migration guide. This is unrelated to missing icons but will cause a completely blank map if omitted. |

---

## 6. Quick checklist for a working dynamic-icon setup (v6)

1. **Import the library as ESM** (`import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` or named imports).  
2. **Create the map** (`new maplibregl.Map({ … })`).  
3. **Call `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()`** *once* (any time after the map instance exists).  
4. Inside the resolver **fetch / generate** the image and **call `map.addImage(id, image)`**.  
5. (Optional) **Cache** the image to avoid duplicate network requests.  
6. **Test** by removing an icon from the sprite sheet or by referencing a non-existent `icon-image` name in your style; the resolver should supply it on-the-fly.

---

### TL;DR code snippet (copy-paste)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: '/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // Try to fetch the icon; fall back to a placeholder.
  try {
    const resp = await fetch(`https://my-icons.example.com/${id}.png`);
    if (!resp.ok) throw new Error('404');
    const img = await createImageBitmap(await resp.blob());
    map.addImage(id, img);
  } catch {
    // placeholder so the layer stays visible
    const placeholder = generatePlaceholderCanvas(id);
    map.addImage(id, placeholder);
  }
});
```

That’s all you need to supply icons dynamically at runtime in MapLibre GL JS v6. Happy mapping!

Below is a minimal, **v6-compatible** way to read everything the public API gives you – center, zoom, bearing, pitch – and, when you really need the raw projection matrix, how to obtain it from a custom layer’s `render` callback (the only place the matrix is exposed publicly).

---

## 1. Public camera getters (always available)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';   // or import {Map} from 'maplibregl'

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
  bearing: 0,
  pitch: 0
});

// Anywhere after the map is loaded you can read the state:
function logCameraState() {
  const center = map.getCenter();          // → LngLat object
  const zoom   = map.getZoom();            // → number
  const bearing = map.getBearing();        // → number (degrees)
  const pitch   = map.getPitch();          // → number (degrees)

  console.log({
    lng: center.lng,
    lat: center.lat,
    zoom,
    bearing,
    pitch
  });
}

map.on('load', logCameraState);
```

These four getters are the **public replacement** for the old `map.transform` properties that disappeared in v6.

---

## 2. Getting the projection matrix

The raw 4 × 4 matrix that transforms world coordinates → screen space is **not exposed on `Map` itself** any more. The only public entry point is the `render` method of a *custom layer*. Inside that method MapLibre passes a `ProjectionData` object that contains the matrix (and a few helper values).

### 2.1. Boiler-plate custom layer

```js
// 1️⃣ Define the layer
const matrixLayer = {
  id: 'matrix-debug',
  type: 'custom',
  renderingMode: '2d',          // or '3d' – both give you the same matrix
  onAdd: function (map, gl) {
    // you can create shaders, buffers, etc. here if you need them
    this.map = map;
  },

  // 2️⃣ This is called every frame after the map has been rendered.
  //    The `render` signature is (gl, matrix) in older docs,
  //    but in v6 the second argument is a `ProjectionData` object.
  render: function (gl, projectionData) {
    // `projectionData` shape (v6):
    // {
    //   matrix: Float32Array(16),          // the combined view-projection matrix
    //   projectionMatrix: Float32Array(16),
    //   viewMatrix: Float32Array(16),
    //   pixelMatrix: Float32Array(16),    // for pixel-perfect calculations
    //   // plus a few convenience numbers:
    //   width, height, zoom, latitude, longitude, bearing, pitch
    // }

    const m = projectionData.matrix;          // Float32Array(16)
    console.log('Projection matrix (column-major):', m);

    // Example: read the matrix into a regular 4×4 array for math libraries
    const mat4 = [
      [m[0],  m[4],  m[8],  m[12]],
      [m[1],  m[5],  m[9],  m[13]],
      [m[2],  m[6],  m[10], m[14]],
      [m[3],  m[7],  m[11], m[15]]
    ];
    // …do whatever custom math you need with `mat4`…

    // (If you don’t actually draw anything, just return.)
  }
};

// 3️⃣ Add the layer after the map is ready
map.on('load', () => {
  map.addLayer(matrixLayer);
});
```

**What you get from `projectionData`:**

| Property | Meaning |
|----------|---------|
| `matrix` | Full view-projection matrix (world → clip space). |
| `projectionMatrix` | The projection part alone (perspective or mercator). |
| `viewMatrix` | Camera view matrix (translation/rotation from center, bearing, pitch). |
| `pixelMatrix` | Matrix that maps world → pixel coordinates (handy for hit-testing). |
| `width` / `height` | Current canvas size (in CSS pixels). |
| `zoom`, `latitude`, `longitude`, `bearing`, `pitch` | Duplicates of the public getters, already baked into the matrix. |

You can use any of those matrices directly in your own math, or combine them with a library like **gl-matrix**, **three.js**, or **mathjs**.

### 2.2. If you only need the matrix once (not every frame)

You can still attach a temporary custom layer, read the matrix in its first `render` call, then remove the layer:

```js
function getProjectionMatrixOnce(callback) {
  const tempLayer = {
    id: '__temp-matrix-layer',
    type: 'custom',
    renderingMode: '2d',
    render: (gl, proj) => {
      callback(proj.matrix);          // give the caller the matrix
      map.removeLayer('__temp-matrix-layer'); // clean up
    }
  };
  map.addLayer(tempLayer);
}

// Usage:
getProjectionMatrixOnce((matrix) => {
  console.log('One-time matrix:', matrix);
});
```

---

## 3. Putting it all together – a helper function

If you frequently need *both* the camera state **and** the matrix, wrap the two steps:

```js
/**
 * Returns a promise that resolves to an object containing
 * center, zoom, bearing, pitch, and the view-projection matrix.
 *
 * @param {maplibregl.Map} map
 * @returns {Promise<{center: maplibregl.LngLat, zoom: number,
 *                    bearing: number, pitch: number,
 *                    matrix: Float32Array}>}
 */
function getCameraStateWithMatrix(map) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const tempLayer = {
      id: '__camera-state-layer',
      type: 'custom',
      renderingMode: '2d',
      render: (_, proj) => {
        resolve({
          center: map.getCenter(),
          zoom: map.getZoom(),
          bearing: map.getBearing(),
          pitch: map.getPitch(),
          matrix: proj.matrix.slice()   // copy if you plan to keep it
        });
        map.removeLayer('__camera-state-layer');
      }
    };
    map.addLayer(tempLayer);
  });
}

// Example usage:
map.once('idle', async () => {
  const state = await getCameraStateWithMatrix(map);
  console.log(state);
});
```

The promise resolves after the next render cycle (i.e., when the matrix is up-to-date). If you call it right after a `setCenter`/`setZoom` etc., you may want to wait for the `moveend` or `render` event first.

---

## 4. Quick recap

| What you need | Public API (v6) |
|---------------|-----------------|
| **Center**    | `map.getCenter()` → `{lng, lat}` |
| **Zoom**      | `map.getZoom()` |
| **Bearing**   | `map.getBearing()` |
| **Pitch**     | `map.getPitch()` |
| **Projection matrix** | Only via a **custom layer** (`render(gl, projectionData)`), where `projectionData.matrix` is the 4 × 4 column-major matrix. No longer accessible through `map.transform`. |

If you need the matrix outside a custom layer (e.g., in a UI component), use the helper shown in §3 to temporarily add a layer, read the matrix, and then remove it.

That’s the complete, v6-compatible recipe for extracting the full camera state you can feed into any custom math you have. Happy coding!

```ts
// 📦  Install the v6 package (pin a major version)
// npm i maplibre-gl@^6

import { Map } from 'maplibre-gl';
import type {
  MapSourceDataEvent,
  MapStyleDataEvent,
} from 'maplibre-gl';

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Helper type-guard – tells TypeScript that the event is a source-data
// event when the `sourceDataType` property exists.
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
function isSourceDataEvent(
  e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent,
): e is MapSourceDataEvent {
  return 'sourceDataType' in e;
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Create the map (make sure you called setWorkerUrl() if you’re in a
// bundler environment – not needed for a plain CDN import)
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
});

// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// Type-safe “data” handler that narrows on `sourceDataType`
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
map.on('data', (e: MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent) => {
  if (isSourceDataEvent(e)) {
    // ✅ This branch is a MapSourceDataEvent – we have access to
    //    `sourceId`, `sourceDataType`, `tile`, etc.
    switch (e.sourceDataType) {
      case 'metadata':
        console.log(`Metadata loaded for source "${e.sourceId}"`);
        break;

      case 'source':
        console.log(`Source "${e.sourceId}" finished loading`);
        break;

      case 'tile':
        // `e.tile` is defined only for tile-related events
        console.log(
          `Tile ${e.tile?.z}/${e.tile?.x}/${e.tile?.y} for source "${e.sourceId}" ${e.tile?.status}`,
        );
        break;

      default:
        // Exhaustiveness check – if a new sourceDataType appears,
        // TypeScript will warn us here.
        const _exhaustive: never = e.sourceDataType;
        console.warn('Unhandled sourceDataType', _exhaustive);
    }
  } else {
    // ✅ This branch is a MapStyleDataEvent – only style-related info
    //    is available (e.g. `styleId`, `isMapStyle` etc.).
    console.log('Style data event:', e);
  }
});
```

### What the code does

1. **Imports** the concrete event types (`MapSourceDataEvent` and `MapStyleDataEvent`) that v6 now exports for the `'data'` event.
2. **Defines a type-guard** (`isSourceDataEvent`) that checks for the presence of the `sourceDataType` property – the only property that distinguishes a source-data event from a style-data event.
3. **Registers** a `'data'` listener on the `Map` instance. Inside the callback:
   * If the event is a `MapSourceDataEvent`, we can safely read `sourceDataType`, `sourceId`, `tile`, etc., and we further narrow with a `switch` on `sourceDataType`.
   * Otherwise the event is a `MapStyleDataEvent`, and we handle it accordingly.

Because the handler’s parameter is typed as the union `MapSourceDataEvent | MapStyleDataEvent`, TypeScript will enforce that you only access properties that exist on the narrowed type, giving you full type safety after the v6 migration.

Below is a minimal, **v6-compatible** way to bootstrap a MapLibre GL JS map in a Vite + TypeScript project that you installed from npm.

```ts
// src/main.ts --------------------------------------------------------------
import { Map, NavigationControl, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

// Vite-specific import that resolves the worker file to a URL.
// The `?worker&url` query tells Vite to treat the file as a Web-Worker
// and return its final public URL (the same URL you would use in a
// plain <script type="module"> import, but Vite can’t infer it automatically).
import mapLibreWorkerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Tell MapLibre where the worker lives.  This **must** be called once
//    (and before any `new Map(...)`) when you bundle with Vite, webpack,
//    esbuild, etc.  The CDN-script version does *not* need this call.
setWorkerUrl(mapLibreWorkerUrl);

// 2️⃣ Create the map instance.
const map = new Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div id="map"></div> in index.html
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
  center: [0, 0],                 // [lng, lat]
  zoom: 2,
  // optional: you can pass any other Map options here (bearing, pitch, etc.)
});

// 3️⃣ Add a UI control (optional, but shows the named-import style).
map.addControl(new NavigationControl());

// -------------------------------------------------------------------------
// OPTIONAL: If your style references icons that are not in the sprite
// sheet, register a resolver instead of the old `styleimagemissing`
// handler (v5 → v6 change).  This is only needed when you really have
// dynamic icons.
map.setMissingStyleImageResolver(async (id) => {
  // Example: generate a simple SVG data-URL on-the-fly.
  const svg = `<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="64" height="64">
                 <circle cx="32" cy="32" r="30" fill="orange"/>
               </svg>`;
  const img = new Image();
  img.src = `data:image/svg+xml;charset=utf-8,${encodeURIComponent(svg)}`;
  await img.decode();               // wait until the image is ready
  map.addImage(id, img);
});
```

### What changed compared with a v5 setup?

| v5 pattern | v6 (this example) |
|------------|-------------------|
| `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` (default export) | `import { Map, NavigationControl, setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl'` (named/namespace import) |
| `require('maplibre-gl')` (CommonJS) | ESM-only import; if you must stay in CommonJS you can `await import('maplibre-gl')` |
| No worker URL handling for bundled builds | **`setWorkerUrl()`** is required; Vite uses `?worker&url` to get the correct URL |
| `map.on('styleimagemissing', …)` + `map.addImage()` resolves the request | `map.setMissingStyleImageResolver()` replaces that pattern |
| Direct access to `map.transform` for zoom/center/etc. | Use public getters (`map.getZoom()`, `map.getCenter()`, …) – not needed in this simple example |
| `MapDataEvent` type | Use `MapSourceDataEvent` / `MapStyleDataEvent` if you type-annotate `data` events |

### Quick checklist for a Vite + TypeScript project

1. **Pin the version** in `package.json` (e.g. `"maplibre-gl": "^6.0.0"`).  
2. Install the CSS (or import it as shown).  
3. Add the `setWorkerUrl()` call **before** any `new Map()`.  
4. Use **named imports** (`{ Map, … }`) – there is no default export in v6.  
5. If you need dynamic icons, replace the old `styleimagemissing` listener with `setMissingStyleImageResolver`.  

That’s all you need to get a working MapLibre GL JS map in a Vite-powered TypeScript app under v6. Happy mapping!

Below is a minimal, **v6-only** example that shows how to:

1. Load a **PMTiles** file (a single-file container for vector tiles).  
2. Register the `pmtiles://` protocol that MapLibre understands.  
3. Add the PMTiles file as a vector source.  
4. Create a simple **line** layer that draws one of the source’s layers.

You can run the snippet as-is in a browser (no bundler required). If you are using a bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.) just move the same imports into your source files and call `setWorkerUrl()` once before creating the map (see the migration guide for that step).

---

## 1️⃣ Install / import the libraries

```html
<!-- CDN – type="module" is required for v6 -->
<script type="module">
  // MapLibre v6 (ESM build)
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@^6.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.mjs';
  // PMTiles library (also ESM)
  import { PMTiles } from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.mjs';

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣ Register the pmtiles:// protocol
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // The PMTiles constructor takes a URL (or a Blob/File) and returns an
  // object that knows how to fetch tiles from that file.
  const pmtilesUrl = 'https://example.com/your-data.pmtiles'; // ← change this
  const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);

  // The library ships a tiny protocol handler that we register with
  // MapLibre’s built-in `addProtocol` helper.
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtiles.tile);

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 3️⃣ Create the map
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',               // id of the <div> below
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  // 4️⃣ When the style is ready, add the PMTiles source + line layer
  // --------------------------------------------------------------
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    // ---- 4a. Add the vector source that points to the PMTiles file ----
    map.addSource('my-pmtiles-source', {
      type: 'vector',
      // The URL must use the protocol we just registered.
      url: 'pmtiles://' + pmtilesUrl
    });

    // ---- 4b. Add a line layer that draws a source layer from the file ----
    // You need to know the name of the layer inside the vector tiles.
    // You can discover it with `pmtiles.listLayers()` or by inspecting the
    // source in a tool like QGIS or tippecanoe’s `--output` metadata.
    const sourceLayerName = 'roads'; // ← change to a layer that exists

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'my-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'my-pmtiles-source',
      'source-layer': sourceLayerName,
      paint: {
        // Simple styling – a thick, blue line
        'line-color': '#0066ff',
        'line-width': 2,
        'line-opacity': 0.8
      }
    });
  });
</script>
```

```html
<!-- The map container -->
<div id="map" style="position: absolute; top:0; left:0; right:0; bottom:0;"></div>
```

---

## 2️⃣ What each part does

| Step | Code | Why it matters |
|------|------|----------------|
| **Import MapLibre v6** | `import * as maplibregl from '…/maplibre-gl.mjs'` | v6 ships only as an ES-module; there is no default export. |
| **Import PMTiles** | `import { PMTiles } from '…/pmtiles'` | The PMTiles library knows how to read the `.pmtiles` container and expose a `tile(z, x, y)` function. |
| **Register protocol** | `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtiles.tile)` | MapLibre’s source URLs can use custom protocols (`pmtiles://`). Registering tells the map how to fetch a tile when it sees that protocol. |
| **Add a vector source** | `type: 'vector', url: 'pmtiles://…'` | The source points to the PMTiles file via the protocol we just added. |
| **Add a line layer** | `type: 'line', source: 'my-pmtiles-source', 'source-layer': 'roads'` | `source-layer` must match a layer name that exists inside the vector tiles. The `paint` block defines the visual style. |

---

## 3️⃣ Tips & Gotchas

| Issue | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| **Don’t know the source-layer name** | ```js\nconst meta = await pmtiles.getMetadata();\nconsole.log(meta.layers); // prints an array of layer names\n``` |
| **CORS errors** | Host the `.pmtiles` file on a server that sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). The PMTiles library uses `fetch()` under the hood, so normal CORS rules apply. |
| **Large files** | PMTiles is designed for random-access reads, but the first request still downloads the file’s header (a few KB). After that each tile is fetched individually. |
| **Bundler (Vite, webpack, etc.)** | After installing `maplibre-gl` and `pmtiles` via npm, import them the same way and **call `setWorkerUrl()` once** before creating the map (see the migration guide). |
| **Multiple PMTiles files** | Register each with its own URL; you can reuse the same protocol name (`pmtiles`) because the handler receives the full URL and resolves the correct file internally. |
| **Styling more than one layer** | Add additional `map.addLayer()` calls, each with its own `source-layer` and styling. |

---

## 4️⃣ Full npm / bundler version (optional)

If you prefer a local development setup:

```bash
npm i maplibre-gl@^6 pmtiles
```

```js
// src/main.ts (or .js)
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from 'pmtiles';
import { setWorkerUrl } from 'maplibre-gl';
import workerUrl from 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl-worker.mjs?worker&url';

// Required for bundlers
setWorkerUrl(workerUrl);

// Register protocol
const pmtiles = new PMTiles('/data/your-data.pmtiles');
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtiles.tile);

// … same map creation code as above …
```

That’s it! With the protocol registered, any `pmtiles://…` URL works as a normal vector source, and you can style it with the full suite of MapLibre layer types (line, fill, circle, symbol, etc.). Happy mapping!
